we're in a period right now that genders  scholars have called the stalled gender  revolution and what this what this means  if you look at all kinds of data what  you see is in the 1970s we started to  see a rapid closing in and narrowing of  gender inequality so the gender wage gap  for example starts to go down rapidly in  the 1970s more women are flooding into  paid labor women are being promoted you  get to the 1980s progress is still being  made although it's a little bit slower  and by the time we hit the mid 1990s  change is kind of flattened out okay so  we're not things aren't getting worse  but we haven't seen a lot of progress in  the move towards gender equality lately  so we're a bit stuck and my take on that  is the reason that we're stuck is that  we haven't effectively changed work  enough to accommodate who's actually in  our workplaces today and that once we do  that we're going to see a jump start  towards the move towards more gender  equality  one thing that we know is stereotypes do  limit women's advancement but they do so  not because people necessarily have  explicit stereotypes about men versus  women but that they have more  unconscious or implicit kind of  associations about what men are like and  what women are like and those implicit  associations then affect how we see  someone's performance how we judge their  behavior and can create sort of slight  differences in the ways that we then  evaluate men and women in the workplace  so these unconscious and explicit biases  I think are one of the biggest barriers  that we're still up against today when  we think about how we can move more  women into leadership often we have to  narrow of a conception of leaders  leaders are these you know autocratic  kind of you know - risk-taking sort of  people and our stereotypes about leaders  overlap with our stereotypes about men  such that men often look naturally more  leader like to us but if we brought in  the stereotypes of leadership that  creates a more expansive view of  leadership and one in which women are  likely to be seen as more successful so  if stereotypes are causing us to see  some people is more talented than they  are and other people is less talented  they are it's interfering with the goal  of being truly meritocratic and is  blocking innovation  what ways can we introduce policies and  procedures and tools into the workplace  that caused people once stereotypes  creep into their head to be aware of  that and stop and move beyond the  stereotype so the goal is rather than  relying on some stereotype about how men  and women are we would instead have some  sort of procedure in place that would  allow us to more effectively evaluate  the true talents and abilities of people  and therefore block the effects of bias  on their judgments we have to help  people first see biases so I do a lot of  work with I'm teaching people about the  way that stereotypes work because until  you see how stereotypes work you're not  going to be aware of how it could be  affecting your judgment and I should  just add just to be clear here is that  these stereotypes affect all of our  judgments women as well as men's we see  that very clearly you'd rarely find that  you know a situation where men are a lot  more bias than women these these are an  unconscious stereotypes we have to help  people see them first so that seeing  black bias is kind of key one of the  things that's been really encouraging to  me in this work is how many really  well-intentioned people there are out  there men and women who you know want to  get beyond bias so the question is can  we help them do that so what I've been  doing is working with a lot of different  companies to help them create tools and  so we have some ideas about the kinds of  things that work in general so for  example we know that when people have  clear criteria for say a promotion  decision that's very clearly articulated  and they read and study that criteria  before they evaluate job applicants  stereotypes are much less likely to  affect their judgments one thing that we  noticed that when people are making  decisions is sometimes the criteria they  use to judge people will shift around  depending upon whether they're  evaluating a woman or a man so a great  example that I that comes from  psychology shows that when people were  evaluating psychology faculty they were  way more likely to say well I would need  to see if she was a good teacher before  I could hire her  but that criteria hadn't been raised  when they were evaluating a man so it's  a perfectly legitimate criteria right  you would want a professor to be a good  teacher  but it was being applied much more  heavily to women than to men so one of  our tools is for someone in the group to  be noticing when new criteria are being  raised and to say something like if  we're going to consider teaching I think  we should go back and consider that for  all of the candidates so that we're  using the same criteria that two blocks  the use of stereotypes so there's a  variety of kinds of things like that  that we can do to not eliminate the  unconscious stereotypes from that are  happening in our head but to block their  negative effects and to be sure that  we're evaluating people by criteria and  not by stereotypes imagine a company  wants to say do more do better on its  diversity and inclusion indicators and  the CEO comes out and says this is one  of our main priorities and we're really  going to work hard on this and that's  great to have that kind of support from  the top is essential but what we find is  that support from the top kind of  trickles down and once you know it's  affecting sort of managers at kind of  higher levels but once it gets to kind  of the mid level of the organization it  often gets stuck so we've got you know  this CEO supporting change at the top  you've got people at the bottom that are  agitating for change but you get to that  middle level and that's what we call the  frozen middle it's a place where  organizational change often gets stuck  especially in diversity and inclusion  and so our goal in working on these  projects is to really try to work at  that frozen middle that is to develop  these tools to block bias by working  directly with people managers and by  working directly with people managers  what we're hoping to do is enlisting  them and the support of creating the  tools helps create buy-in at that level  where change otherwise gets stuck  there's something that's actually really  quite easy to do and that is we can  share the accomplishments of other women  in our networks at work one thing that  we know is that stereotypes cause women  to be seen as less competent than they  actually are and that's what limits  women's advancement women can't fix this  problem themselves right you can't as a  woman you know if people aren't noticing  your accomplishments you can't just say  I'm brilliant  for example there'll be all kinds of  backlash directed at you but one thing  that we can do is tout other women's  accomplishments so I think being very  mindful of giving attention to the  accomplishments of women in the  organization is hugely important so  individuals can do this organizations as  well I think you know announcing through  Department newsletters and things like  that the accomplishments of their women  employees are crucially important for  raising those women's status and fahrt  starting to fracture the stereotypes  that we have that are leading us to  cause us to see women as less competent  than they are
 

